<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuomboka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuomboka ('to get out of the water') is the annual ceremony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, held near the end of the rainy season when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> floods the Bulozi plain. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litunga">Litunga</a> travels in the royal barge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalikwanda">Nalikwanda</a>, paddled by dozens of men to the sound of royal drums, from his floodplain capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lealui">Lealui</a> to his dry-season residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limulunga">Limulunga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuomboka.jpg" alt="Kuomboka" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dietmar Hatzenbichler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nalikwanda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tradition dates the ceremony to the 17th century and a great flood called meyi a lungwangwa, when people were afraid to escape in their small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a>. The high god Nyambe ordered a man named Nakambela to build the first great canoe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalikwanda">Nalikwanda</a>, meaning for the people, and the people escaped the flood. The date of each year's ceremony was set by the height of the water and the phase of the moon; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litunga">Litunga</a> first consulted the Royal Graves, and then the royal Maoma drums were sounded to call the paddlers.</p><p>The first barge, Njonjolo, was made of reeds, but barges were soon built of wood, reportedly first by the Litunga Mboo. Each Litunga gave his barge a praise name: Mboo's was Sitandamwalye, Inyambo's Lyamashandi and Ngalama's Notila, and Yeta II, who used Notila, also built one called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalikwanda">Nalikwanda</a>. Tradition credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulambwa">Mulambwa</a>, said to have ridden an elephant to become heir, with making the elephant a royal symbol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Maoma drums sound around the capital the day before. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litunga">Litunga</a> sets out in traditional dress and during the journey changes into the full uniform of a British admiral, presented in 1902 by King Edward VII in recognition of the treaties between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. Two white scout canoes go ahead to test the depth of the water and look for enemies; once they signal all clear, the royal barge follows, with further boats for the Litunga's wife and for the Prime Minister, who travels with the local chiefs known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/induna">Indunas</a>. The journey normally takes six to eight hours.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalikwanda">Nalikwanda</a> is painted black and white and carries a large black elephant whose ears can be moved from inside, and a fire whose smoke tells the people that the king is alive and well. Its paddlers, for whom the task is a great honour, wear scarlet berets with a piece of lion's mane and knee-length skirts of animal skins. The three royal war drums, kanaona, munanga and mundili, each more than a metre wide, keep the paddlers in time. The wife's barge bears a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> egret, Nalwange, whose wings move like the elephant's ears. The return journey, the less publicised Kufuluhela, is usually made in August.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuomboka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court moved each year as the flood rose: in the <b>Kuomboka</b> ceremony the Litunga travels in the royal barge Nalikwanda, paddled by dozens of men, from the floodplain capital to a residence on higher ground, today from Lealui to Limulunga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
